		<title>QuantHealth Raises $45M to Let Pharm Test Clinical Trials with AI Before Spending Billions</title>
		<link>https://ventureburn.com/quanthealth-raises-45m-series-b-ai-clinical-trial-simulations/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Ekemini]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 14:45:52 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Startup]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI agents]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[artificial intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[clinical trials]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[QuantHealth]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://ventureburn.com/?p=203970</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Drug development is a gamble. More than 90% of drugs that enter clinical trials fail. Three out of four of those failures are because the drug didn’t work or wasn’t</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/quanthealth-raises-45m-series-b-ai-clinical-trial-simulations/" data-wpel-link="internal">QuantHealth Raises $45M to Let Pharm Test Clinical Trials with AI Before Spending Billions</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Drug development is a gamble. More than 90% of drugs that enter clinical trials fail. Three out of four of those failures are because the drug didn’t work or wasn’t safe. And by the time you find out, you’ve already spent years and hundreds of millions.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Israeli startup QuantHealth </span><a href="https://lnkd.in/p/eQg6cgnm" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener external noreferrer" data-wpel-link="external"><span style="font-weight: 400">raises $45M</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400"> in Series B to change that math. The round was led by Qumra Capital, with Sanofi Ventures, Pitango HealthTech, Bertelsmann, Accenture Ventures and others joining. Total funding is now $75M. The idea is to simulate the trial before you run it.</span></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">The Problem That QuantHealth Solves</span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Right now, pharma makes clinical development decisions the traditional way. They design a trial, enroll patients and wait for months or even years for data. If it works, they adjust with time, and then repeat the entire process. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">While that process works, it is slow, expensive, and unforgiving. With costs rising and competition tightening, companies are under pressure to get drugs to market faster and fail faster when something won’t work. That’s where QuantHealth comes in.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/valar-atomics-raises-1-billion-integrated-nuclear/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Valar Atomics Raises $1B To Build Vertically Integrated Nuclear Firm</i></b></a></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">How QuantHealth Works </span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">QuantHealth built an AI platform that lets drugmakers run thousands of simulated trials before enrolling patients. Instead of relying on historical benchmarks and subjective assumptions, the platform models how a specific trial design would likely play out. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">It tests different protocols, spots risks around efficacy and safety, and helps teams optimize before committing capital. CEO Orr Inbar puts it plainly, “Today, decisions are still made through real-world iteration. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">We’re fundamentally changing that model.” The tech already has traction. QuantHealth says it has simulated more than 600 clinical trials across 30 medical indications with predictive accuracy of up to 90%. 12 of the world’s top 20 pharmaceutical companies are using it to design trials and cut risk.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The new capital is going into three things:</span></p>
<ol>
<li style="font-weight: 400"><b>Better models:</b><span style="font-weight: 400"> Develop next-generation AI and increase datasets so simulations get sharper.</span></li>
<li style="font-weight: 400"><b>Broader coverage: </b><span style="font-weight: 400">Expand from 30 to 40+ indications, with focus on oncology, cardiometabolic, and inflammatory diseases.</span></li>
<li style="font-weight: 400"><b>Full lifecycle: </b><span style="font-weight: 400">Move beyond trial design into commercial planning, so the platform helps from lab to launch.</span></li>
</ol>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">They’ll also grow the team. QuantHealth has about 85 people in Tel Aviv and New York today.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/ore-energy-raises-43-million-series-a-for-iron-air-batteries/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Ore Energy Raises $43M To Build Lasting Rust Batteries</i></b></a></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">The QuantHealth Team</span></h2>
<div id="attachment_203972" style="width: 1034px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-203972" class="size-large wp-image-203972" src="https://ventureburn.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Quant_health-Founders-1024x683.jpg" alt="" width="1024" height="683" /><p id="caption-attachment-203972" class="wp-caption-text">QuantHealth Founders</p></div>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Founded in 2020 by Orr Inbar and Arnon Horev. Inbar came from ConcertAI. Horev ran business ops at Nucleai and led sales at EarlySense. They both observed how pharma makes billion-dollar decisions with insufficient data. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Early backers included Sanofi and Accenture Ventures. This round adds more strategic pharma and healthcare investors, which matters because adoption in this industry is all about trust.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Clinical trials are the most expensive and uncertain part of drug development. If you can predict failure in simulation, you save years and billions. The broader trend is clear: pharma is moving from “try it and see” to “predict it first.” </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Predictive technologies are becoming core infrastructure, not nice-to-have tools. If QuantHealth’s 90% accuracy holds at scale, it could reshape how drugs are developed. There’ll be fewer failed late-stage trials, faster timelines, and more shots on goal for new therapies. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The risk is the same as any AI-in-healthcare company: can you prove the simulations match reality across different diseases, populations, and regulators? That’s what the next phase is for. With $45M, QuantHealth is betting it can.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/june-ai-raises-20-million-in-pre-seed-funding-to-automate-enterprise-ai-deployment/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>June AI Raises $20M To Automate Enterprise AI Deployment</i></b></a></p>
<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/quanthealth-raises-45m-series-b-ai-clinical-trial-simulations/" data-wpel-link="internal">QuantHealth Raises $45M to Let Pharm Test Clinical Trials with AI Before Spending Billions</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
			</item>
